<title>CANADA: Canadian foreign reserves rise in December.</title>
<headline>Canadian foreign reserves rise in December.</headline>
<dateline>OTTAWA 1997-01-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Canadian foreign reserve holdings rose in December, the Finance Department said on Monday.</p>
<p>   RESERVES			  Dec 96	Nov 96</p>
<p>   Total			   20.578 bln  20.116 bln</p>
<p>   U.S. dollars		  17.521 bln  17.040 bln</p>
<p>   Other foreign currencies     507 million     501 million</p>
<p>   Gold				 155 million     156 million</p>
<p>   Special drawing rights     1.168 bln   1.176 bln</p>
<p>   IMF reserves		   1.227 bln   1.243 bln</p>
<p>Note - Figures in U.S. dollars.  </p>
<p>Official government operations decreased reserves by US$671 million.</p>
<p>Details of the changes in the month:</p>
<p>    Gain on gold sales		    --</p>
<p>    Earnings on investments	    +97</p>
<p>    Revaluation effects		   -9</p>
<p>    Foreign currency debt	   +1,054</p>
<p>    Other transactions		    -9</p>
<p>NOTE - in millions of U.S. dollars  </p>
<p>During December, Canada bills decreased by $35.7 million to a level of outstanding bills of $5.055 billion. Sales of Canada notes totalled $90 million, increasing the level of outstanding notes to $1.525 billion. Foreign currency debt was also increased by long-term cross-currency swaps of domestic obligations, which raised $1.0 billion.</p>
<p>((Reuters Ottawa Bureau (613) 235-6745))</p>
</text>
